hey guys welcome to biology professor  today I'm going to be summarizing some  news articles about how red pandas are  two species not one I've seen this  research been summarized in a few  different publications now but I'm  mainly going off of one that I read in  Newsweek the original research was  published and in the journal called  scientific excuse me science advances  and so what did they do to determine  that red pandas are made up of two  species instead of just one well we've  got a ton a team of Chinese researchers  they use whole genome sequencing so that  means that they extracted the DNA from  cells from 65 different red pandas from  different populations across the red  panda range they were mainly looking at  Y chromosome DNA and mitochondrial DNA  that's the DNA that we inherit from our  father and mother respectively so they  looked at seven separate populations and  determined that there were two separate  species represented there that separated  some time around 220,000 years ago so  we've got the Himalayan red panda and  the Chinese red panda and they do have  distinct ranges and so you might be  asking was this surprising and the  answer is not really  it had kind of already been expected for  a while they were already considered two  different subspecies and they also  differ in appearance for example the  Chinese red panda has like a rudder face  and has more distinct bands on its tail  but now we finally have the genetic  analysis to back it up to show us that  these two different subspecies are  actually two different species and so  you might be asking well how did they  form into two species well like I said  it happened about 220,000 years ago and  the two separate species kind of went  their own ways when they were separated  by a river so this is a form of  allopatric speciation if you are  interested in learning more about  allopatric speciation versus sympatric  speciation you can check out my video on  those topics but basically allopatric  speciation speciation is when you have  two different species that are  geographically  separated in some way either by a river  as in the case of these pandas or  perhaps there is you know deforestation  that separates them into two different  forested areas with an unfortunate area  between them or like a gorge forms for  example there are two different species  of I think it's squirrel one that lives  on the north side of the Grand Canyon  when it lives on the south side of the  Grand Canyon and they were separated by  the river forming that Canyon you know  millions of years ago and eventually did  did form into separate species  so that's allopatric speciation  sympatric speciation is when you have  species that diverged despite being in  the same location so again you can check  out my video on that topic if you're  interested in learning more about it so  what else so these researchers learn  about the red pandas first you need to  know what a population bottleneck is a  population bottleneck is when you have a  really sharp reduction in population  size over a really short period of time  this can be caused by a variety of  things for example if a if an infectious  disease wipes through a species and  kills 80% of the individuals of those  species then that would be a population  bottleneck because when 20 percent left  something like like drought or famine or  a new predator coming into an area or  climate change basically anything that  has a sudden impact on a species  resulting in having its numbers sharply  decrease that's a population bottleneck  and it's important because when you have  a population bottleneck it typically  reduces the genetic variety of the  species by a large degree so if we go  back to my previous example if you had  80 percent of the individuals of a  species die because of infectious  disease the 20 percent that are  remaining will probably have less  genetic diversity so less variety in the  types of genes and the types of genetic  mutations that they have which means  that that species might not be a stable  any  and so what they learned is that the  Himalayan red pandas have experienced  three bottlenecks and only a small  expansion afterwards meaning that this  species has lower numbers and lowered  genetic diversity whereas the Chinese  red pandas as the other species has  experienced only two population  bottlenecks and a larger population  expansion so it has higher genetic  variety and the larger population size  so of the two of them the Himalayan red  pandas would be the ones that are most  at risk and so why is all this research  important well the red pandas are  already endangered even if they were  just one species that one species has  low enough numbers that it's already  considered endangered this is due to  habitat loss poaching as their numbers  have dwindled there's been a lot of  inbreeding and so you have a situation  where you've got only a few thousand red  pandas left and losing more every day  and so this research now that we can  realize that there are actually two  separate species those two different  species can have individual conservation  plans developed for them so they can  have the individual protection plans and  conservation biologists can work with  zoos with governments with locals to  make sure that a different the two  different species of red panda are both  being protected and it will also prevent  crossbreeding in zoos so you won't have  a Himalayan red panda and a Chinese red  panda being you know being mates in a  zoo situation because now zoo keepers  will realize that they are separate  species so that's it for today what do  you guys think of the red pandas they're  they're cute little animals their faces  are a little rodent II for me but  otherwise they're really pretty really  pretty red fur and so what do you think  about the fact that they are two  different species and what this means  for conservation efforts let me know in 